# ---------------- Fluids ----------------
rev('fluids-a', [('What a Pitot tube measures', "Water stalls at the mouth of the tube, so its velocity head turns into extra height in the column. The difference between the stagnation and static columns is v^{2} / 2g, and the handbook's Pitot formula is Bernoulli's equation already solved for v."),
    ('One reading is not the mean', "Flow is slower near the bed and walls, so a single velocity measured mid-stream overstates the mean. A correction factor, or several readings, is needed before multiplying by area."),
    ('Orifice coefficients', "The discharge coefficient combines contraction of the jet and friction. For a sharp-edged orifice it is about 0.61. The handbook table is listed under the orifice's description, such as sharp-edged or submerged.")])
rev('fluids-b', [('Numbers to know', "Water: 1,000 kg/m^{3}, 9,810 N/m^{3}, 62.4 lb/ft^{3}. Specific weight divided by density is g. A density in g/mL times 1,000 is in kg/m^{3}.")])
rev('fluids-c', [('Layered liquids', "Pressure at depth is the sum of density × g × thickness for each layer above. The pressure diagram for a lower layer starts at the pressure of the interface, not at zero."),
    ('Force on a wall from the pressure diagram', "The force on a vertical wall is the area of its pressure diagram times the wall's width. Split the diagram into rectangles and triangles; a triangle's resultant acts two thirds of the way down from its apex."),
    ('Centre of pressure on an inclined surface', "Measure along the slope: y_{cp} = y_{c} + I / (y_{c} A), with the moment of inertia taken for the dimension that runs down the slope. Convert between vertical depth and slant distance with the sine of the angle. The shift below the centroid is small for a small gate at depth."),
    ('Barometers and floating loads', "A barometer column balances atmospheric pressure: p = specific weight × height. A raft can carry a load equal to the weight of water it could displace minus its own weight.")],
    ["In the centre of pressure formula, dividing I by y_{c} alone. It is divided by the product y_{c} A."])
rev('fluids-d', [('Use gauge pressure in the energy equation', "Atmospheric pressure appears on both sides and cancels, so work in gauge pressure. An outlet open to the air is at zero. Put the datum at the lower point so one elevation term vanishes."),
    ('Thrust on a pipe bend', "The pressure force p A acts along the pipe at each end of a bend. Because the two act in different directions they leave a resultant, 2 p A sin(angle / 2) when velocities are low, which a thrust block must resist. The block's bearing area is the thrust divided by the allowable soil pressure.")],
    ["Using p / density where the pressure head p / specific weight is needed."])
# ---------------- Surveying ----------------
